rows.reply:
Under SGB V §10, children of a member of statutory health insurance are themselves insured through Familienversicherung (family cover for dependants under statutory insurance). This applies to the member's children if they meet all five conditions in §10(1):
1. they have their residence or habitual abode in Germany ("ihren Wohnsitz oder gewöhnlichen Aufenthalt im Inland haben");
2. they are not insured in their own right under § 5(1) nos. 1 to 8, 11 to 12, and are not voluntarily insured;
3. they are not exempt from insurance and have not been released from the obligation to insure;
4. they are not self-employed as their main occupation;
5. they have no total income regularly above one seventh of the monthly reference figure under § 18 of SGB IV.

Children are covered up to their 18th birthday (§10(2) no. 1). §10(2) nos. 2 to 4 extend this: to 23 if not in work, to 25 if in school or vocational training or a voluntary social or ecological year, and with no age limit for children with disabilities who cannot support themselves.

There is an exception in §10(3). Children are not insured this way if all of the following apply to the parent's spouse or civil partner who is related to the child:
- that parent is not a member of a Krankenkasse;
- their total income regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings ceiling ("Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze") per month;
- their income is regularly higher than the member's.
The excerpts do not say how a child is insured in that case.

If the conditions are met through more than one member, the member chooses the Krankenkasse (§10(5)).

The step you take is a report. The member must report the insured child to the responsible Krankenkasse, with the information needed to carry out the Familienversicherung, and must also report later changes (§10(6)). The national association of health insurance funds sets a uniform procedure and uniform forms for this report. The excerpts do not give a deadline for it.

You will need the birth certificate to register the child with the Krankenkasse. You must register the birth within one week at the Standesamt responsible for the place of birth, and the Standesamt issues the birth certificate. If the child is born in a hospital, birth centre or maternity clinic, the facility notifies the Standesamt and sends it the birth notification. After a home birth, you must take the birth notification to the Standesamt within one week yourself.

The excerpts do not say whether your child meets these conditions. Since the member's Krankenkasse receives the report, that is where to ask about your own child's cover. In many maternity clinics, Lotsinnen and Lotsen (hospital guides) also explain benefits and help in practical ways with filling in applications. The service is free and voluntary.
